1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,241
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,241 --> 00:00:07,107
This is chapter eleven: Java 9 to 11.

3
00:00:07,557 --> 00:00:14,089
Most teams skipped straight through this range to Java 11, the long-term release everyone ran.

4
00:00:14,089 --> 00:00:17,842
So its features are widely used and rarely understood.

5
00:00:17,842 --> 00:00:18,885
Let's fix that.

6
00:00:19,785 --> 00:00:23,124
Java 9 to 11 fixed three unrelated pains.

7
00:00:23,124 --> 00:00:26,545
First, the classpath had no encapsulation.

8
00:00:26,545 --> 00:00:32,654
Anything public was reachable, including JDK internals never meant for you.

9
00:00:32,654 --> 00:00:37,705
Libraries built on them, so every JDK upgrade broke something.

10
00:00:38,155 --> 00:00:41,369
Second, every deployment shipped the whole runtime.

11
00:00:41,369 --> 00:00:45,088
Over two hundred megabytes for a service that needed forty.

12
00:00:45,538 --> 00:00:56,247
And third, HTTP meant the clunky HttpURLConnection or an extra library, with no HTTP/2 and no async.

13
00:00:57,147 --> 00:01:02,664
A module declares what it needs and what it exposes, in a module-info file.

14
00:01:02,664 --> 00:01:04,209
Requires and exports.

15
00:01:04,659 --> 00:01:07,438
That gives two rules instead of one.

16
00:01:07,438 --> 00:01:10,990
Readability: A can use B only if A requires B.

17
00:01:10,990 --> 00:01:16,782
Accessibility: a public type in B is visible only if B exports its package.

18
00:01:16,782 --> 00:01:18,867
Public is no longer enough.

19
00:01:19,317 --> 00:01:25,152
Most applications never write a module-info, and that's a defensible choice.

20
00:01:25,152 --> 00:01:30,450
But you can't opt out of the fact that the JDK itself is modularised.

21
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:34,826
Its internals are now behind boundaries the JVM enforces.

22
00:01:35,726 --> 00:01:38,014
Here's what that feels like.

23
00:01:38,014 --> 00:01:44,713
Java 16 strongly encapsulated JDK internals, and Java 17 removed the escape hatch.

24
00:01:44,713 --> 00:01:55,417
So a library that reflects into java dot util now fails with an InaccessibleObjectException, even in an app that never modularised.

25
00:01:55,867 --> 00:01:58,291
The add-opens flag grants access.

26
00:01:58,291 --> 00:02:00,936
But treat it as a temporary measure.

27
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:07,843
Each one is a library reaching into JDK internals, and the real fix is upgrading that library.

28
00:02:07,843 --> 00:02:13,573
A startup script that collects add-opens flags over the years is a to-do list.

29
00:02:14,023 --> 00:02:15,754
And know the difference.

30
00:02:15,754 --> 00:02:19,432
Exports lets other code compile against your types.

31
00:02:19,432 --> 00:02:25,778
Opens permits deep reflection, which is what JPA, Jackson and dependency injection need.

32
00:02:26,678 --> 00:02:31,482
Here's the module-system benefit most teams actually collect.

33
00:02:31,482 --> 00:02:34,868
jdeps lists the JDK modules your jar needs.

34
00:02:34,868 --> 00:02:38,411
jlink builds a runtime containing only those.

35
00:02:38,861 --> 00:02:44,637
The result is about forty-five megabytes instead of a two-hundred-megabyte JDK.

36
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:48,659
That's smaller container layers and faster cold starts.

37
00:02:48,659 --> 00:02:52,095
And it works for a plain classpath application.

38
00:02:52,095 --> 00:02:54,947
jlink builds the runtime, not your app.

39
00:02:55,847 --> 00:03:04,839
Java 11 standardised a modern HTTP client, with HTTP/2 by default and both blocking and async calls.

40
00:03:04,839 --> 00:03:11,313
Build a request with a URI, headers, a timeout and a body, then send it.

41
00:03:11,763 --> 00:03:18,334
The client is immutable and thread-safe, so keep one per application in a static final field.

42
00:03:18,334 --> 00:03:21,726
Creating one per request leaks connection pools.

43
00:03:22,176 --> 00:03:24,108
And notice the two timeouts.

44
00:03:24,108 --> 00:03:28,593
Connect timeout on the client only covers opening the connection.

45
00:03:28,593 --> 00:03:31,767
The request timeout covers the whole exchange.

46
00:03:31,767 --> 00:03:35,424
Set only the first, and a hung read can wait forever.

47
00:03:36,324 --> 00:03:39,747
var is type inference, resolved at compile time.

48
00:03:39,747 --> 00:03:45,311
The variable still has exactly one static type, and the bytecode is identical.

49
00:03:45,311 --> 00:03:53,156
It helps when the right-hand side already names the type, like a constructor call, or a loop over map entries.

50
00:03:53,606 --> 00:03:55,956
It hurts when the type disappears.

51
00:03:55,956 --> 00:03:59,895
var result equals process input tells the reader nothing.

52
00:03:59,895 --> 00:04:05,354
And here's the real trap: var lines equals new ArrayList with an empty diamond.

53
00:04:05,354 --> 00:04:11,505
There's nothing to infer from, so you get an ArrayList of Object, and every add compiles.

54
00:04:11,955 --> 00:04:13,188
So use this test.

55
00:04:13,188 --> 00:04:16,672
Can a reader name the type from that line alone?

56
00:04:16,672 --> 00:04:20,083
If not, var has removed information, not noise.

57
00:04:20,983 --> 00:04:23,134
Some small APIs worth using.

58
00:04:23,134 --> 00:04:34,892
Java 9's collection factories, List dot of, Set dot of and Map dot of, give you truly immutable collections, not a read-only view over something mutable.

59
00:04:35,342 --> 00:04:39,208
They reject nulls, not only on creation but on lookup.

60
00:04:39,208 --> 00:04:42,787
Even asking whether the list contains null throws.

61
00:04:43,237 --> 00:04:52,539
And Set dot of and Map dot of deliberately randomise their iteration order on every JVM run, so your code can't depend on it.

62
00:04:52,989 --> 00:05:00,715
Java 11 added isBlank, strip, repeat and lines on strings, plus Files dot readString and writeString.

63
00:05:00,715 --> 00:05:07,218
Prefer strip over trim, because trim predates Unicode and misses non-breaking spaces.

64
00:05:08,118 --> 00:05:12,668
Three things actually break when you move off Java 8.

65
00:05:12,668 --> 00:05:17,304
First, NoClassDefFoundError on javax dot xml dot bind.

66
00:05:17,304 --> 00:05:21,683
Java 11 removed JAXB and the other Java EE modules.

67
00:05:21,683 --> 00:05:25,718
Add them back as ordinary Jakarta dependencies.

68
00:05:26,168 --> 00:05:30,814
Second, InaccessibleObjectException from reflection libraries.

69
00:05:30,814 --> 00:05:34,936
Upgrade the library, using add-opens only as a stopgap.

70
00:05:35,386 --> 00:05:41,151
Third, anything using sun dot misc dot Unsafe or com dot sun internals.

71
00:05:41,151 --> 00:05:48,378
Find the supported replacement, usually VarHandle or the foreign function and memory API.

72
00:05:48,828 --> 00:05:53,832
Modularising a service itself is optional, and usually not worth it.

73
00:05:53,832 --> 00:06:01,927
But if you ship a library, publish an Automatic-Module-Name, so consumers who do modularise get a stable name.

74
00:06:02,827 --> 00:06:04,089
Production rules.

75
00:06:04,089 --> 00:06:07,951
One static final HttpClient, with both timeouts set.

76
00:06:07,951 --> 00:06:10,847
var only where the line names the type.

77
00:06:10,847 --> 00:06:15,452
List dot of and Map dot of for constants, and strip over trim.

78
00:06:15,452 --> 00:06:18,497
Use jdeps and jlink for container images.

79
00:06:18,497 --> 00:06:24,587
And treat every add-opens flag as a ticket, with a note on which library needs it.

80
00:06:25,037 --> 00:06:27,906
Never create an HttpClient per request.

81
00:06:27,906 --> 00:06:30,701
Never write var with an empty diamond.

82
00:06:30,701 --> 00:06:36,659
Never assume the new factories tolerate null, or depend on their iteration order.

83
00:06:36,659 --> 00:06:42,765
And never split a package across modules, which fails at launch on the module path.

84
00:06:43,665 --> 00:06:49,830
Java 9 brought modules, the collection factories, private interface methods and JShell.

85
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:52,008
Java 10 brought var.

86
00:06:52,458 --> 00:07:05,045
Java 11, the long-term release, standardised HttpClient, added the string and file helpers and single-file source launch, and removed the Java EE modules.

87
00:07:05,495 --> 00:07:10,380
Java 16 and 17 closed off JDK internals for good.

88
00:07:10,830 --> 00:07:17,550
With Java 21's virtual threads, the simple blocking send call is the natural style again.

89
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,888
And Java 26 adds HTTP/3 to the client.

90
00:07:21,888 --> 00:07:24,037
That's chapter forty.

91
00:07:24,937 --> 00:07:25,643
To recap.

92
00:07:25,643 --> 00:07:32,226
The JDK is modularised, so strong encapsulation applies whether you opted in or not.

93
00:07:32,226 --> 00:07:36,771
Exports is for compiling against, opens is for reflection.

94
00:07:36,771 --> 00:07:40,847
jlink shrinks your runtime, even for classpath apps.

95
00:07:40,847 --> 00:07:43,825
Keep one HttpClient with two timeouts.

96
00:07:43,825 --> 00:07:47,508
And use var only where the line names the type.

97
00:07:47,958 --> 00:07:52,007
Next is chapter twelve: records, sealed types and text blocks.

98
00:07:52,007 --> 00:07:53,313
Thanks for watching.
